swehouse.c: Porphyry cusps 11 and 12 grow from the Ascendant, not the MC

Porphyry trisects each ecliptic quadrant, so every cusp is the Ascendant or the
MC plus a fixed fraction of the quadrant. Cusps 11 and 12 are offsets from the
MC, and their speeds were built from ac_speed.

The correction needs no reference value, because the fix makes two identities
hold that the geometry already requires:

    speed[11] = mc' + (ac' - mc')/3  = (ac' + 2 mc')/3  = speed[3]
    speed[12] = mc' + 2(ac' - mc')/3 = (2 ac' + mc')/3  = speed[2]

After the patch cusp 11 reads 426.9100065 and so does cusp 3; cusp 12 reads
513.8188284 and so does cusp 2. Before it neither pair matched. That is the
whole argument, and it takes half a minute to confirm.

Four cusp speeds change, not two. swehouse.c mirrors cusp_speed[5] onto 11 and
cusp_speed[6] onto 12, so 5 and 6 move with them.

    swetest -b1.1.2000 -ut12:00 -house13.4,52.5,O -speed

      house  5  151 55  6.2512   687 38 11.2999   ->  426 54 36.0233
      house  6  191 45  6.1539   774 32 43.0587   ->  513 49  7.7822
      house 11  331 55  6.2512   687 38 11.2999   ->  426 54 36.0233
      house 12   11 45  6.1539   774 32 43.0587   ->  513 49  7.7822

The new values are the rates at which those cusps' own positions move; the old
ones were not. Cusps 5 and 11 change by 38 per cent, 6 and 12 by 34, to numbers
working code may already be reading, which is the reason to look at the
demonstration before applying it.

Five house systems fall back to Porphyry inside the polar circle and inherit its
cusp speeds, so the same defect shows through any of them there. Koch makes it
easiest to see, because the sign changes:

    swetest -b1.1.2000 -ut12:00 -house13.4,80,K -speed

      house 11  309 24 43.3718  -367 21 23.5702   ->  163  9 42.3058

The old value had that cusp moving the wrong way round the zodiac. This is
Porphyry's defect seen through Koch, not Koch's own; Koch's own patch shows
below the polar circle, not above it.

Positions are untouched.

This is one concern lifted out of "swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds
analytically" so that it can be taken alone. That patch contains this change and
rewrites the surrounding code as well, so the two cannot both be applied; it is
also available as an eight-step series, of which this is one step. Independent of
every other patch in the set.

---
 swehouse.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 5f750e4..668752a 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -1470,6 +1470,11 @@ porphyry:
     hsp->cusp[3] = swe_degnorm(hsp->ac + (180 - acmc) / 3 * 2);
     hsp->cusp[11] = swe_degnorm(hsp->mc + acmc / 3);
     hsp->cusp[12] = swe_degnorm(hsp->mc + acmc / 3 * 2);
+    /* Porphyry trisects each ecliptic quadrant equally, so every cusp is the
+     * Ascendant or the MC plus a fixed fraction of the quadrant acmc and the
+     * speeds follow directly. Cusps 11 and 12 are offsets from the MC and
+     * grow from mc_speed; before this patch they grew from ac_speed, which
+     * was wrong. */
     if (hsp->do_hspeed) {
       double q1_speed = hsp->ac_speed - hsp->mc_speed;	// rate of growth of quadrant 1
       // double q4_speed = hsp->mc_speed - hsp->ac_speed;	// rate of growth of quadrant 4
@@ -1477,8 +1482,10 @@ porphyry:
       hsp->cusp_speed[10] = hsp->mc_speed; // dito
       hsp->cusp_speed[2] = hsp->ac_speed  - q1_speed / 3;
       hsp->cusp_speed[3] = hsp->ac_speed  - q1_speed / 3 * 2;
-      hsp->cusp_speed[11] = hsp->ac_speed  + q1_speed / 3;
-      hsp->cusp_speed[12] = hsp->ac_speed  + q1_speed / 3 * 2;
+      /* cusps 11 and 12 are offsets from the MC, not from the Ascendant,
+       * so they grow from mc_speed. q1_speed is d(acmc)/dt. */
+      hsp->cusp_speed[11] = hsp->mc_speed  + q1_speed / 3;
+      hsp->cusp_speed[12] = hsp->mc_speed  + q1_speed / 3 * 2;
     }
     break;
   case 'Q':	/* Pullen sinusoidal ratio */
